{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-130202",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, recall that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{s\\to 2}f(s)=⟨{lim}_{s\\to 2}\\frac{5}{{e}^{s-2}},\\,{lim}_{s\\to 2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4-2s),\\,{lim}_{s\\to 2}\\,({s}^{3}-5)⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one by one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 2}\\frac{5}{{e}^{s-2}} & =\\frac{5}{{e}^{2-2}}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4-2s) & =3{lim}_{s\\to 2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4-2s) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4-2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 2}\\,({s}^{3}-5) & ={2}^{3}-5=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{s\\to 2}f(s)=⟨5,3,3⟩.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
